  "query_preview": "..The upper-flow pattern this morning is defined by\na strong subtropical high centered over Chihuahua Mexico and a\ntrough of low pressure along the Pacific Northwest coastline and\na baggy trough axis south of this feature along the West Coast.\nThe upper trough is expected to dig thru the Pacific Northwest \ninto the northern Great Basin by 17/00Z this afternoon. As this \noccurs, the mean trough axis will nudge east into southern CA \nresulting in a shunting of the subtropical high away from us\ntoward the SE. With the shifting pattern aloft, the 850-700mb \ntheta-e ridge that was residing across Cochise and southern \nGraham Wednesday will shift east resulting in a general drying \nfrom west to east...reducing the potential for heavy rainfall to\nextreme SE Cochise this afternoon. A pocket of dry air west of \nTucson in the latest CIRA Advected Layer Precipitable Water \n(ALPW) imagery between 850-700mb will move also east today, \nundercutting the mid-level moisture above it. This will \nincrease the potential for evaporation within the lower-levels \nof storm downdrafts today thus resulting in stronger \nthunderstorm outflows to 40-45 mph across Cochise county with\nareas of blowing dust possible. This is supported by the 26/00Z\nHREF which indicated a 70-90% probability of outflow winds 30+ \nkts this afternoon across the eastern half of Cochise. \nOtherwise, there will be a general decrease in the overall \ncoverage of storms across Southeast Arizona, with the majority \nof the activity limited to the east of Tucson.\n\nOtherwise, the mean trough continues to dig SE into the southern\nGreat Basin this weekend resulting in strong and gusty southwest\nwinds across Southeast Arizona. Although the stronger mid-level\nwinds will be across the northern half of the state, we will see\ntight gradients resulting in southwest 20-foot winds 15 to 25\nmph with gusts 35-40 mph. Given the dry airmass in place with\nminimum RHs 10-15 percent, we do have a Red Flag Warning in\neffect for the majority of Southeast Arizona for both Saturday \nand Sunday. Breezy conditions will continue Monday with \nelevated to locally near critical fire weather conditions. \n\nThat pesky trough then begins to relax and retreat NW the \nsecond half of next week, resulting in the upper ridge building \nback thru New Mexico and into ERN AZ. This should help pump \nmoisture back into the CWA from the E/SE Thursday and Friday, \njust in time for the more typical ramp up of Monsoon activity \naround the 4th of July.",
